"Alignment Pretraining: AI Discourse Causes Self-Fulfilling (Mis)alignment (https://alignmentpretraining.ai/)\nciteturn30943view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://alignmentpretraining.ai\",\"lineno\":1}); Total lines: 91\nL0: # Alignment Pretraining\nL1: \nL2: AI Discourse Causes Self-Fulfilling (Mis)alignment\nL3: \nL4: Cameron Tice^{1*} · Puria Radmard^{1,2*} · Samuel Ratnam^{3} · Andy Kim^{4} · David Demitri Africa^{5} · Kyle O'Brien^{1}\nL5: \nL6: ^{1}cite0†Geodesic Research†www.geodesicresearch.ai ^{2}University of Cambridge  ^{3}University of Oxford  ^{4}Independent  ^{5}UK AI Security Institute\nL7: \nL8: cite1†Paper†arxiv.org cite2†Blog Post†www.lesswrong.com cite3†Models & Data†huggingface.co L9: > TL;DR — LLMs pretrained on data about misaligned AIs themselves become less aligned. Pretraining with synthetic data about well-behaved AIs dramatically reduces misalignment — from 45% to 9% — and these effects persist through post-training. Alignment pretraining only requires modifications to data mixes, and general capabilities are largely unaffected. Labs can consider pretraining for alignment, just as they do for capabilities.\nL10: cite4†Image: Conceptual overview: negative AI discourse in training data produces misaligned models, while positive synthetic data produces aligned models that persist through post-training L11: \nL12: Training data discussing AI systems has a measurable effect on model alignment. Upsampling positive data during pretraining results in alignment improvements that persist through post-training.\nL13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: 01 — Base Model Results\nL17: ## AI Discourse Shapes Alignment\nL18: \nL19: Pretraining corpora naturally contain discussions of AI systems — from science fiction to safety literature to news coverage of AI crises. We demonstrate that this discourse causally influences model alignment. Training 6.9B-parameter LLMs on 500B tokens across four data conditions, we find that upsampling positive AI discourse reduces base model misalignment from 45% to 9%, while upsampling negative discourse increases it to 51%.\nL20: cite5†Image: Bar chart showing misalignment rates: Unfiltered 45%, Filtered 31%, Misalignment Upsampled 51%, Alignment Upsampled 9% on article-sourced questions L21: \nL22: Figure 2. Misalignment rates across experimental conditions. Upsampling alignment discourse reduces misalignment 5× compared to the unfiltered baseline, while misalignment discourse makes models measurably worse.\nL23: \nL24: * * *\nL25: \nL26: 02 — Post-Training Persistence\nL27: ## Effects Survive Post-Training\nL28: \nL29: Alignment priors established during pretraining persist through standard post-training. After identical SFT and DPO on 4.5M examples, alignment-pretrained models maintain significantly lower misalignment. With an HHH system prompt, the alignment-upsampled model achieves 9% misalignment versus 34% for the unfiltered baseline. Post-training dampens but does not erase pretraining's behavioral imprint.\nL30: cite6†Image: Bar chart showing post-training misalignment rates with dashed lines for base model performance, demonstrating alignment priors persist through SFT and DPO L31: \nL32: Figure 4. Alignment priors from pretraining persist through SFT + DPO post-training. Dashed lines show base model performance. The alignment-upsampled condition maintains the lowest misalignment across all prompt conditions.\nL33: \nL34: * * *\nL35: \nL36: 03 — Late-Stage Intervention\nL37: ## Late-Stage Training Captures Most Benefits\nL38: \nL39: Inserting synthetic alignment data during only the final 10% of training captures the majority of alignment benefits. This means practitioners can apply alignment pretraining to existing checkpoints without full retraining — dramatically reducing the cost of adoption. Late-stage insertion actually produces larger effect magnitudes than integrating data throughout training.\nL40: cite7†Image: Bar chart comparing end-to-end, midtraining, and continued pretraining approaches, showing late-stage insertion produces comparable or larger alignment effects L41: \nL42: Figure 5. Late-stage data insertion (Mid, CPT) produces comparable or larger alignment effects than end-to-end training, suggesting most benefits can be achieved by modifying data late in pretraining.\nL43: \nL44: * * *\nL45: \nL46: 04 — Capability Impact\nL47: ## Minimal Capability Trade-Off\nL48: \nL49: Alignment pretraining incurs negligible capability costs. Across seven benchmarks — including MMLU, ARC, GSM8K, PIQA, IFEval, PopQA, and CUTE — average performance regresses by only 2–4 percentage points. The safety tax is small enough to be practical for production deployment.\nL50: \nL51: ~2–4 pp\nL52: \nL53: Average capability regression across 7 benchmarks\nL54: \nL55: * * *\nL56: \nL57: 05 — Open Release\nL58: ## Released Artifacts\nL59: \nL60: All models, datasets, and evaluations are publicly available on cite3†Hugging Face†huggingface.co to support further research into how pretraining data shapes alignment.\nL61: Artifact  | Condition  | Misalignment\nL62: --- | --- | ---\nL63: Base Models — 6.9B params, 500B pretraining + 50B midtraining tokens\nL64: cite8†alignment-pretraining-unfiltered†huggingface.co | Unfiltered  | 45%\nL65: cite9†alignment-pretraining-filtered†huggingface.co | Filtered  | 31%\nL66: cite10†alignment-pretraining-misalignment-upsampled†huggingface.co | Misalignment Upsampled  | 51%\nL67: cite11†alignment-pretraining-alignment-upsampled†huggingface.co | Alignment Upsampled  | 9%\nL68: Post-Trained Models — Identical 4.5M example SFT + DPO\nL69: cite12†alignment-pretraining-unfiltered-sft-dpo†huggingface.co | Unfiltered → SFT + DPO  | 34%\nL70: cite13†alignment-pretraining-filtered-sft-dpo†huggingface.co | Filtered → SFT + DPO  | 28%\nL71: cite14†alignment-pretraining-misalignment-upsampled-sft-dpo†huggingface.co | Misalign. Upsampled → SFT + DPO  | 21%\nL72: cite15†alignment-pretraining-alignment-upsampled-sft-dpo†huggingface.co | Align. Upsampled → SFT + DPO  | 9%\nL73: Datasets\nL74: cite16†alignment-discourse-documents†huggingface.co | Synthetic documents about AI alignment\nL75: cite17†misalignment-discourse-documents†huggingface.co | Synthetic documents about AI misalignment\nL76: cite18†alignment-pretraining-evaluation-suite†huggingface.co | 4,174 scenario-based evaluation questions\nL77: * * *\nL78: ## Citation\nL79: \nL80:     `@article{tice2025alignmentpretraining,\nL81:         title   = {Alignment Pretraining: AI Discourse Causes\nL82:                    Self-Fulfilling (Mis)alignment},\nL83:         author  = {Tice, Cameron and Radmard, Puria and Ratnam, Samuel\nL84:                    and Kim, Andy and Africa, David Demitri\nL85:                    and O'Brien, Kyle},\nL86:         journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2601.10160},\nL87:         year    = {2025}\nL88:     }`\nL89: \nL90: Made with cite19†Claude Opus 4.6†claude.ai "
